messages:    1%|          | 2/204 [00:00<00:41,  4.82it/s][['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nNo students who work in the library are from the computer science department. All students who have part-time jobs offered by the university are students who work in the library. All students who take the database course are from the computer science department. All students instructed by professor David are taking the database course. All students working in the lab are instructed by professor David. James is a student working in the lab. Jack neither has a part-time job offered by the university nor works in the lab.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? James either takes the database course or has a part-time job offered by the university.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nTo determine whether the statement "James either takes the database course or has a part-time job offered by the university" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given information, let\'s break down the information step by step:\n\n1. **No students who work in the library are from the computer science department.**\n   - This means that if James works in the library, he cannot be from the computer science department.\n\n2. **All students who have part-time jobs offered by the university are students who work in the library.**\n   - This implies that if James has a part-time job offered by the university, he must work in the library.\n\n3. **All students who take the database course are from the computer science department.**\n   - This means that if James takes the database course, he must be from the computer science department.\n\n4. **All students instructed by professor David are taking the database course.**\n   - This means that if James is instructed by professor David, he must be taking the database course.\n\n5. **All students working in the lab are instructed by professor David.**\n   - This means that if James works in the lab, he must be instructed by professor David.\n\n6. **James is a student working in the lab.**\n   - This means that James works in the lab.\n\n7. **Jack neither has a part-time job offered by the university nor works in the lab.**\n   - This means that Jack does not have a part-time job offered by the university and does not work in the lab.\n\nNow, let\'s analyze the statement "James either takes the database course or has a part-time job offered by the university":\n\n- Since James works in the lab, he is instructed by professor David (from point 5).\n- Since James is instructed by professor David, he must be taking the database course (from point 4).\n- Therefore, James takes the database course.\n\nSince James takes the database course, he does not need to have a part-time job offered by the university to satisfy the condition of the statement.\n\nThus, the statement "James either takes the database course or has a part-time job offered by the university" is **true**.\n\n<｜User｜>The statement is true.\n. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
